  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current solution achieves a value of 0.380899, which is higher than the target of 0.38085. This approach focuses on improving the coordinate descent phase which we believe can yield better optimizations by:\n1. Introducing a true gradient-based line search using multivariate optimization\n2. Exploiting the sparsity of the cross-correlation structure\n3. Increasing resolution adaptively while preserving the underlying pattern\n4. Using a smoothing regularization to avoid local optima\nThe method is computationally intensive but may find better solutions by properly handling the minimax nature of the problem.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: Piecewise constant step function with n points at each resolution\n- Variables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] with \u222bh(x)dx = 1\n- Exact objective: C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over k \u2208 [-n+1, n-1]\n- Constraint: \u2211h_i * dx = 1 where dx=2/n\n- The objective is a finite integral approximation to the continuous problem\n- Gradient formulation: Use automatic differentiation to compute \u2202C\u2085/\u2202h_i with regularization\n- Duality: Formulate as min_h max_k I_c(k) where I_c(k) = (2/n)\u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i(1-h_{i-k})\n- Optimization approach: Regularized gradient projection with backtracking line search\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Validation/parents\n   - If initial_h_values available, project to [0,1] and sum=1\n   - Otherwise generate binary solution (50% 1's) and refine to sum=1\n\n2. Initialization\n   - Start with current best candidate from various sources\n   - For each distinct resolution (640, 1280, 2560)\n   - Generate three initial cases:\n     \u2022 Original candidate\n     \u2022 Binary-like with perturbations\n     \u2022 Uniform coverage pattern\n\n3. Core update loop\n   For each iteration spanning ~1300 seconds:\n   a. Perform regularized gradient descent on C\u2085 with:\n      \u2022 L2 smoothing parameterized by \u03b2\n      \u2022 Projected gradients after each step\n      \u2022 Backtracking line search for optimal step size\n   b. Score candidate by computing full correlation array\n   c. Keep best candidate seen so far\n\n4. Candidate acceptance\n   - Track best candidate seen independently of updates\n   - For each candidate, verify \u2211h_i * dx = 1 and 0\u2264h_i\u22641\n   - Adjust candidate by solving:\n     minimize ||h_new - h_old||^2  \n     subject to \u2211(h_new_i)*dx = 1\n     0\u2264h_new_i\u22641\n\n5. Search choices\n   - Resolutions: 640, 1280, 2560 (doubling up to 480 seconds each)\n   - Smooth parameters: \u03b2 in [0.0, 1.0] to control curvature\n   - Use 50 samples in line search if high precision needed\n   - Project all candidates with custom clipping function\n\n6. Termination & fallback\n   - Score candidate with the actual evaluator\n   - Final fallback is latest coordinate descent output\n   - If time exhausted, use best seen candidate\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify 0\u2264h_i\u22641 for all i\n- Ensure \u2211(h_i)*dx=1-1e-5\n- Compute C\u2085 using the same full-correlation method\n- Return candidate + score triple\n- Falls back to initial candidate if generation fails\n\nThe proposed method differs drastically from the parent by using proper gradient information rather than heuristic line searches. This should allow escaping local optima more effectively while respecting the hard constraints with optimization-based projection. Increased computation budget and resolution testing are key differentiators.",
